{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b73823f74856713a","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8002\n3\tepic_count: 8\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8002 — Integrate Kueue Workload Scheduling Information in Workbenches Overview\n11\t\n12\t## Decomposition Approach\n13\t\n14\tThe Staff Engineer Input takes precedence over the AI-generated strategy sections. Key inputs applied:\n15\t\n16\t- **Component scope**: Only `odh-dashboard` (frontend) is affected. The rhods-operator HardwareProfile mutating webhook that injects the `kueue.x-k8s.io/queue-name` label is explicitly out of this strategy's scope (Staff input override — the AI TL;DR's mention of rhods-operator-injected behavior is context only).\n17\t- **Scope constraints override the pre-refinement \"Proposed Solution\" section**: quota/capacity preview (LocalQueues carry no quota), estimated wait-time predictions, rich \"Kueue Insights\" admin visualization, feature-flag model changes, and opt-in/opt-out controls are all out of scope. Only the eleven committed HLRs are decomposed.\n18\t- **Non-blocking UX dependency**: Development must not block on RHOAIUX-7470 (visual treatment and content review). Engineering ships placeholder visual treatment and default message copy; UXD and Content streams run in parallel and their deliverables replace placeholders when ready. The placeholder replacement itself is owned by a dedicated follow-on integration epic (E008) so it is explicit scope rather than an unowned coordination task.\n19\t\n20\t**Component name mapping (canonical list)**: The canonical RHAI component list has no `odh-dashboard` entry; the closest canonical name is `AI Core Dashboard`, used for all dashboard-code and dashboard-content epics. The UXD design epic uses the canonical `UXD` component; the documentation epic uses `Documentation`.\n21\t\n22\t**No Investigation epics.** Both open questions were evaluated against the decision rule (does the answer change which downstream epics exist or what they do?):\n23\t- *Visibility API RBAC aggregation* (`batch-user`/`view`/`edit` aggregating `pending-workloads-lq-viewer-role`): the implementation is identical either way — display position when authorized, silently omit on 403. Reclassified as a validation acceptance criterion on the Visibility API epic.\n24\t- *Form of the positive Kueue indicator* (banner vs. badge vs. label): the strategy mandates placeholder-driven development with minimal engineering effort regardless of choice. Reclassified as the UXD design epic's deliverable plus an interim-treatment acceptance criterion on the indicator epic.\n25\t\n26\t## Epic List\n27\t\n28\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n29\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n30\t| RHAISTRAT-8002-E001 | State-driven Kueue workload status model (eviction reasons, requeue state, message overrides) | Implementation | Dashboard UI | P0 |\n31\t| RHAISTRAT-8002-E002 | Kueue management indicators (positive project indicator, non-Kueue workload anomaly indicator) | Implementation | Dashboard UI | P0 |\n32\t| RHAISTRAT-8002-E003 | Kueue Visibility API integration for queue position display | Implementation | Dashboard UI | P1 |\n33\t| RHAISTRAT-8002-E004 | HardwareProfile queue UX (GitOps popover fallback, LocalQueue name validation, dropdown filtering explanation) | Implementation | Dashboard UI | P1 |\n34\t| RHAISTRAT-8002-E005 | UX design and content review for Kueue visibility indicators (RHOAIUX-7470) | Implementation | UXD | P1 |\n35\t| RHAISTRAT-8002-E006 | Status message microcopy overrides for Kueue workload conditions | Implementation | Content | P1 |\n36\t| RHAISTRAT-8002-E007 | Documentation: release note and feature-docs determination for Kueue workbench visibility | Implementation | Docs | P1 |\n37\t| RHAISTRAT-8002-E008 | Apply finalized RHOAIUX-7470 designs and reviewed status message copy (placeholder swap-in) | Implementation | Dashboard UI | P1 |\n38\t\n39\t## Dependency DAG\n40\t\n41\t```mermaid\n42\tgraph TD\n43\t    E001[\"E001 Status model (P0)\"] --> E007[\"E007 Documentation (P1)\"]\n44\t    E002[\"E002 Kueue indicators (P0)\"] --> E007\n45\t    E003[\"E003 Visibility API position (P1)\"] --> E007\n46\t    E004[\"E004 HardwareProfile queue UX (P1)\"] --> E007\n47\t    E005[\"E005 UXD design (P1)\"] --> E007\n48\t    E006[\"E006 Content microcopy (P1)\"] --> E007\n49\t    E001 --> E008[\"E008 Placeholder swap-in (P1)\"]\n50\t    E002 --> E008\n51\t    E005 --> E008\n52\t    E006 --> E008\n53\t    E008 --> E007\n54\t```\n55\t\n56\t## DAG Justification\n57\t\n58\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n59\t|------|------|-----------|\n60\t| E001 → E007 | Rule 11 (docs-last) | Documentation is blocked by all Implementation epics; docs describe what was built. |\n61\t| E002 → E007 | Rule 11 (docs-last) | Same. |\n62\t| E003 → E007 | Rule 11 (docs-last) | Same. |\n63\t| E004 → E007 | Rule 11 (docs-last) | Same. |\n64\t| E005 → E007 | Rule 11 (docs-last) | Same. |\n65\t| E006 → E007 | Rule 11 (docs-last) | Same. |\n66\t| E008 → E007 | Rule 11 (docs-last) | Same — documentation is blocked by the follow-on integration epic as well. |\n67\t| E001 → E008 | Rule 13 (artifact consumer) | The swap-in loads reviewed copy into the data-driven override table mechanism E001 builds; it cannot complete before that mechanism exists. |\n68\t| E002 → E008 | Rule 13 (artifact consumer) | The swap-in applies finalized visual treatments to the presentation-isolated indicator elements E002 builds. |\n69\t| E005 → E008 | Rule 13 (artifact consumer) | The swap-in applies the finalized design specifications that are E005's deliverable. |\n70\t| E006 → E008 | Rule 13 (artifact consumer) | The swap-in loads the reviewed microcopy lookup table that is E006's deliverable. |\n71\t| *No edges among E001–E004* | Rule 15 (same repo, different areas → parallel) | All four dashboard epics live in odh-dashboard but touch distinct sub-systems (status derivation pipeline, namespace/label-driven indicator elements, Visibility API client, HardwareProfile form/popover). Merge-conflict risk is coordination, not dependency. |\n72\t| *No UXD/Content → implementation edges (E001–E004)* | Strategy dependency note | The strategy explicitly mandates that development must NOT be blocked on UX answers; the core implementation epics E001–E004 use placeholder visual treatment and default copy and run fully parallel to the UXD/Content streams. The only UXD/Content → downstream edges point at E008, the follow-on swap-in epic whose very purpose is to apply the finalized deliverables — that is the replacement step the strategy itself describes (\"replace when UX delivers\"), not development blocked on UX answers. |\n73\t| *No UXD ↔ Content edge* | Rule 14 (independent streams) | Design and microcopy streams feed the same review (RHOAIUX-7470) but produce independent deliverables. |\n74\t\n75\t**Docs-authoring priority exception applied**: E007 derives P1 from the strategy frontmatter priority (`Major` → P1), not from priority inheritance; the P0 implementation epics do not push P0 onto it.\n76\t\n77\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n78\t\n79\tHLRs are numbered in strategy order (HLR-1 … HLR-11).\n80\t\n81\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement (abbreviated) | Epic(s) |\n82\t|-----|----------|---------------------------|---------|\n83\t| HLR-1 | P0 | Distinguish Preempted from other Eviction reasons in workbench status | E001 |\n84\t| HLR-2 | P0 | Surface Requeued state with retry count and next retry time from `requeueState` | E001 |\n85\t| HLR-3 | P0 | Positive Kueue indicator on project details page (`kueue.openshift.io/managed=true`) | E002 |\n86\t| HLR-4 | P0 | Anomaly indicator on non-Kueue workloads in Kueue-managed namespaces | E002 |\n87\t| HLR-5 | P0 | Workload scheduling status from Workload CR conditions, human-friendly overrides + raw fallback | E001 |\n88\t| HLR-6 | P1 | Kueue Visibility API queue position (`PendingWorkloadsSummary` on LocalQueue), graceful 403 degradation | E003 (partial: `positionInLocalQueue` only) |\n89\t| HLR-7 | P1 | HardwareProfile popover fallback for GitOps-managed notebooks (direct queue label, source indication) | E004 |\n90\t| HLR-8 | P1 | `k8sNameRegex` validation on LocalQueue name field in HardwareProfile admin form | E004 |\n91\t| HLR-9 | P1 | Info indicator on HP dropdown explaining queue-enabled filtering | E004 |\n92\t| HLR-10 | P2 | Structured, extensible status derivation model (away from wildcard/substring event matching) | E001 |\n93\t| HLR-11 | P2 | Status message granularity: WaitForRecovery vs. WaitForStart, AdmissionCheck pending/retry | E001 (engineering), E006 (wording) |\n94\t\n95\tFull coverage: all 11 HLRs map to at least one epic. E005, E007 and E008 are support streams (design, documentation, placeholder swap-in) and do not own HLRs; E005 enables the finalized visual treatment of HLR-3/HLR-4, E006 owns the wording deliverable behind HLR-5/HLR-11's override table, and E008 applies those finalized deliverables in place of the engineering placeholders. HLR-6 is covered partially and deliberately by E003: `positionInLocalQueue` is implemented; `positionInClusterQueue` is excluded because the ClusterQueue endpoint is cluster-scoped and admin-only, and the strategy's definitive acceptance criteria require only local-queue position.\n96\t\n97\t**Priority-bundling decisions**:\n98\t- HLR-10 and HLR-11 (P2) are bundled into the P0 status-model epic E001 rather than split: HLR-10 (structured, state-driven derivation) is the foundation the P0 eviction/requeue/status HLRs build on — the Staff input's architecture constraints mandate state-driven derivation for exactly the P0 behaviors. HLR-11 distinguishes message states on the same override surface. All five HLRs share the same data structures (`WorkloadCondition` types, Workload CR conditions) and the same UI surface (workbench status display); splitting would force two epics to build the same status pipeline plus an artificial serialization edge.\n99\t- HLR-7/8/9 (all P1) share the HardwareProfile form/dropdown/popover surface — one epic, no deferrable split.\n100\t\n101\t## Health Warnings\n102\t\n103\t- **RBAC open question (non-blocking)**: Whether data scientists hold `view`/`edit`/`batch-user` bindings that aggregate Kueue's `pending-workloads-lq-viewer-role` is unresolved. Code handles 403 gracefully either way; if the role is absent, queue position is effectively admin-only. Tracked as a validation AC on E003 and owned by AI Core Platform (per the strategy's open-questions table), not as a DAG gate.\n104\t- **Placeholder-swap follow-up (owned by E008)**: Placeholder visual treatment (E002) and default message copy (E001) ship ahead of RHOAIUX-7470 deliverables (E005/E006). Applying the finalized designs/copy is a small follow-on integration task on the dashboard, owned by E008; the engineering epics carry ACs ensuring the override table and indicator elements are structured so the swap-in needs no rework. The residual risk is scheduling (E008 waits on the UXD/Content streams), not missing scope.\n105\t- **Pre-refinement content superseded**: The strategy's upper \"Proposed Solution\"/\"User Stories\" sections describe resource-quota columns, estimated wait times, and historical queue data. These are superseded by Staff input scope constraints (out of scope) and are intentionally not covered by any epic. Anyone reading the strategy top-down should use the HLR list as definitive.\n106\t- **Priority note**: E005/E006/E008 are P1 despite serving P0 features' final polish — the strategy explicitly makes the UX stream non-blocking, so priority inheritance does not apply; E008 is downstream of (not a prerequisite for) all P0 work.\n107\t\n108\t## Tiered Delivery\n109\t\n110\tNot applicable. There is no external dependency with uncertain timing gating user value: the only external-ish dependency (UXD/content via RHOAIUX-7470) is explicitly non-blocking, with placeholder-driven engineering as Tier-independent parallel work. All implementation epics deliver value independently.\n111\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b90076d6a4b1a6ed","agent_id":"a7cd0b3b14fbc947b","session_id":"6e556f5d-8f87-4575-9eb4-8b9f55533407","uuid":"89d1397d-734d-4edd-b9a9-f139397fc0e0","timestamp":"2026-10-22T06:18:50.077Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b73823f74856713a","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review revised RHAISTRAT-8002"}
